## Pre-specified GSEA ranking metric (item 3, fixed before any enrichment was seen)
metric = shrunken log2FC x (-log10 RAW Wald p)  (equivalently sign(lfc)*|lfc|*(-log10 p))
Why: the p<0.05 / |lfc|>1 / baseMean>10 filter already handles significance and effect-size
thresholds, but the ranked list must still order genes by both; a pure log2FC ranking lets a
huge-effect low-count gene outrank a well-measured moderate one. The pure-shrunken-log2FC ranking is
also written (out/ranked_KLvsWL_log2fconly.tsv) because this choice can change the top pathway.
Handling rules (deterministic, so permutations are reproducible):
  p == 0 (float underflow, 5 genes) floored to 1e-300 -> -log10 p = 300, no infinite weights;
  NaN p / NaN SE dropped (5,984 genes, the all-zero ones); baseMean < 1 excluded;
  ties broken by (shrunken log2FC desc, geneID asc);
  duplicate symbols (many Ensembl IDs -> one symbol) collapsed by MEAN of the metric; unmapped genes
  excluded from enrichment but retained in the CSVs; gene sets screened at min_size 15 / max_size 500.
Measured impact of this choice: Spearman(metric, shrunken log2FC) = 0.998, but only 173/200 of the
top-200 genes are shared -> 27 of the top 200 differ, i.e. the metric choice is not cosmetic.

## KL3 / normalisation sensitivity (item 4)
Robustness of normalisation: re-estimating size factors from expressed genes only (baseMean>=10
reference set) gives KL1..WL3 = 1.034,1.045,0.821,1.030,1.080,1.013 -> DE = 720 vs 722,
Jaccard 0.983, Spearman 1.000. So the 722-gene set is NOT an artefact of the normalisation choice.
Leave-one-out (each subset re-normalised, re-dispersed, re-shrunk, re-filtered):
  drop KL3  DE=967  Jaccard 0.607  Spearman 0.910  lambda 4.30
  drop KL1  DE=596  Jaccard 0.760  Spearman 0.969  lambda 3.30
  drop KL2  DE=595  Jaccard 0.747  Spearman 0.976  lambda 3.28
  drop WL3  DE=1009 Jaccard 0.601  Spearman 0.937  lambda 4.38
  drop WL1  DE=394  Jaccard 0.541  Spearman 0.976  lambda 2.61
  drop WL2  DE=391  Jaccard 0.533  Spearman 0.974  lambda 2.63
Per-sample deviation from the other two in its group (median |log2 dev|, baseMean>10):
  KL3 0.479 > WL3 0.340 > KL1 0.262 ~ KL2 0.256  -> KL3 is the noisiest sample, as its size factor
  (0.816) suggested.
Interpretation: gene-level ordering is stable (Spearman 0.91-0.98), but the DE COUNT is not
(Jaccard 0.53-0.76; 391-1009 genes depending on which single sample is dropped). The 722-gene set
should therefore be read as an approximate DE set, not as a stable gene list; any downstream
enrichment conclusion would need DESeq2/limma and ideally more replicates.

